  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: DiscRC 4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "A research team wants to use stored patient tissue samples to study
    a specific cancer mutation. You make sure the research team has the right approvals
    and patient permissions to use those samples first.\n\n<b>How does the research
    coordinator\u2019s role differ from a lab researcher\u2019s role at this stage?</b>"
  explanation: "In early discovery, lab researchers carry out the hands-on scientific
    work\u2014studying tissue samples, analyzing cancer cells, and identifying new
    mutations. Research coordinators, on the other hand, help ensure that any patient
    samples or data used in those experiments follow proper ethical, legal, and privacy
    standards. While research coordinators aren\u2019t running lab experiments themselves,
    they make sure research teams follow the rules and keep things organized from
    the very start."
  correctAnswer: The research coordinator focuses on approvals and proper use of
    patient samples, while the lab researcher runs tests on the samples in the lab
  incorrectAnswer1: The research coordinator runs laboratory tests, while the lab
    researcher reviews patient consent forms
  incorrectAnswer2: The research coordinator decides which cancer mutations the researchers
    will study, while the lab researcher gathers patient tissue samples
  incorrectAnswer3: None of these answers
  questionRole: Research Coordinator
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
